<main class="lux-article-page"><nav class="lux-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en', '')); ?>">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/blog', '')); ?>">Knowledge Blog</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Italian Food Label Dictionary: PDO, PGI, Organic and Extra Virgin</span></nav><article class="lux-reader-layout lux-academy-reader">
    <div class="lux-reader-rule"></div>
    <section class="lux-reader-hero"><div class="lux-reader-hero-copy"><div class="lux-reader-meta-grid"><span>Italian Food Dictionary</span><span>Italian Food Dictionary · 2026</span></div><h1 id="lux-reader-title">Italian Food Label Dictionary: PDO, PGI, Organic and Extra Virgin</h1><p class="lux-reader-summary">Italian food packaging often contains terms and symbols that can appear highly technical. The important question is not how premium they look. It is what they actually mean.</p></div><figure class="lux-reader-cover"><img width="2000" height="1333" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/dictionary-food-labels.webp'); ?>" alt="Italian Food Label Dictionary: PDO, PGI, Organic and Extra Virgin"></figure></section>
    <section class="lux-reader-content"><aside class="lux-reader-aside"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/blog', '')); ?>">Back to Knowledge Blog</a></aside><div class="lux-reader-copy"><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-0"><h2>Protected Designation of Origin</h2><p>The European Protected Designation of Origin system protects products with a particularly close relationship to a defined geographical area.</p><p>Production requirements are established through an official product specification.</p><p>A protected geographical name therefore represents much more than simply “made in Italy.”</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-1"><h2>Protected Geographical Indication</h2><p>Protected Geographical Indication is another European geographical protection system.</p><p>The relationship between product and territory follows different requirements from Protected Designation of Origin, but it is likewise an official system rather than a private marketing badge.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-2"><h2>Traditional Speciality Guaranteed</h2><p>Traditional Speciality Guaranteed protects traditional characteristics, composition, or production methods.</p><p>Unlike geographical indications, the focus is primarily on the traditional product or method rather than requiring every stage to be restricted to one narrowly defined place.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-3"><h2>Organic</h2><p>Organic claims on Italian and European food products operate within the European organic regulatory system.</p><p>Organic does not automatically mean:</p><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>completely pesticide-free;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>nutritionally superior;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>completely unprocessed.</li></ul><p>It means production must comply with the applicable organic rules and certification requirements.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-4"><h2>Extra Virgin Olive Oil</h2><p>“Extra virgin” is not simply a premium adjective invented by a brand.</p><p>It is a formal olive-oil category.</p><p>Extra virgin olive oil must comply with specified production, chemical, and sensory requirements.</p><p>“Olive oil” and “extra virgin olive oil” are therefore not identical product categories.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-5"><h2>Cold Extraction</h2><p>“Cold extraction” may appear alongside extra virgin olive oil, but the two terms do not mean the same thing.</p><p>Extra virgin describes the oil category.</p><p>Cold extraction is an additional production claim that may be used when the relevant legal conditions are met.</p><p>An oil does not cease to be extra virgin simply because “cold extraction” is absent from the front label.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-6"><h2>Made in Italy</h2><p>“Made in Italy” provides useful information about origin or production, but it does not explain everything about the food.</p><p>Consumers should continue looking at:</p><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>specific territory;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>producer;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>ingredient origin;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>product category;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>ingredients;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>allergens;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>recommended use.</li></ul><p>Country of origin is the beginning of understanding a product, not the end.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-7"><h2>Ingredients Matter More Than Marketing Adjectives</h2><p>Packaging can use words such as:</p><p>“selected”</p><p>“traditional”</p><p>“premium”</p><p>“classic”</p><p>“Italian style”</p><p>These expressions may communicate positioning, but they cannot replace formal product information.</p><p>A useful reading order is:</p><p>official product name → category → ingredients → allergens → origin → nutrition → storage → usage.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-8"><h2>LuxurEat(露意膳) Perspective</h2><p>LuxurEat(露意膳) aims to help consumers do more than simply trust an Italian-looking package.</p><p>Product pages should combine cultural storytelling with clear factual information.</p><p>Origin, tradition, and culinary use explain the story.</p><p>Ingredients, allergens, category, storage, and traceability explain the product.</p><p>Long-term trust comes from both.</p></section></div></section>
